${PKGSTEM}

Initial native OpenBSD adaptation
=================================
This port still requires OpenBSD privilege, Tor and clock-lifecycle testing.
Do not enable it on a production machine before completing those tests.
The Python network process is unprivileged; only a small C parent can change
the clock. No executable is setuid, and no doas.conf rule is required.

Tor configuration (administrator action)
========================================
The existing tor package uses _tor and /var/tor, with data mode 0700.
Leave that directory private. Add the following to ${SYSCONFDIR}/tor/torrc:

    SocksPort 127.0.0.1:9050
    ControlPort 127.0.0.1:9051
    CookieAuthentication 1
    CookieAuthFile /var/run/tor-sdwdate/control.authcookie
    CookieAuthFileGroupReadable 1

First create the cookie's separate directory:

    install -d -o _tor -g _tor -m 0750 /var/run/tor-sdwdate
    rcctl restart tor

Ensure that this directory is recreated before Tor at boot if local runtime
directory cleanup removes it. Inspect ownership and mode of the resulting
cookie; it must be _tor:_tor and group-readable, never world-readable.
Check ${SYSCONFDIR}/tor/torrc for conflicting existing settings before editing.
No configuration of Tor or system accounts is silently modified by this port.

The C launcher gives its child exactly the _sdwdate and _tor groups. Thus the
child can read this cookie, but /var/tor stays inaccessible. SAFECOOKIE is
mandatory. This grants full Tor-controller authority, not a read-only subset;
consider a dedicated Tor instance if other applications depend on this Tor.
Never expose the control port publicly or disable its authentication.

All configured time URLs must be v3 onion HTTP(S) URLs. Proxy addresses must
be literal loopback IPs. requests ignores proxy environment variables, uses
socks5h for both schemes, verifies HTTPS normally, and refuses redirects.
There is no clearnet fallback. A severely wrong clock can prevent Tor or HTTPS
from bootstrapping; repair it deliberately rather than weakening validation.

Configuration and operation
===========================
The unchanged upstream pools are installed as a sample in
${SYSCONFDIR}/sdwdate.d/30_default.conf. Put overrides in 50_user.conf;
files are read in name order and are not executed. Supported native additions:

    CONTROL_PORT=9051

PROXY_IP, PROXY_PORT, MAX_FAILURE_RATIO, RANDOMIZE_TIME and the pool syntax
retain upstream meanings, subject to the local-only proxy restriction.
Keep configuration root-owned and not writable by _sdwdate.

First inspect base ntpd; never let two services independently change the clock:

    rcctl check ntpd
    rcctl check tor

For intentional clock-changing tests on an isolated OpenBSD machine:

    rcctl disable ntpd
    rcctl stop ntpd
    rcctl enable sdwdate
    rcctl start sdwdate

These are administrator actions, not package installation actions. The rc
script refuses startup while any ntpd process is present. It cannot prevent
someone starting ntpd later or another privileged program changing the clock.
Direct invocation bypasses that rc check: verify ntpd manually first.

A safe initial foreground run, as root, is:

    ${TRUEPREFIX}/sbin/sdwdate --dry-run --once

This runs network selection under _sdwdate but never changes the clock or
replay checkpoint. For persistent dry-run service testing:

    rcctl set sdwdate flags --dry-run
    rcctl start sdwdate

Remove that flag explicitly only when ready for controlled clock tests.
Status is JSON in /var/run/sdwdate/status (mode 0600). Operational messages
use syslog daemon facility and stderr. Upstream detailed fetch diagnostics
also use stdout; run foreground when collecting those details.

The first successful correction can step at most 24 hours; --slew-only
forbids it. Subsequent corrections are limited to +/-30 seconds, delivered
as at most 5ms per second. Larger corrections are rejected, not converted
into a step. Completion may take up to 100 minutes plus scheduler delays.
Queries then sleep for upstream's randomized 60-180 minute interval.
On SIGTERM/EOF/crash of the broker no further incremental correction occurs.
Already completed adjustments are not reversed. No Linux hwclock command
is installed or invoked; kernel/RTC persistence must be checked natively.

The replay checkpoint is written atomically only after successful completion.
Its initial floor is the selected release date; upstream's 2033 upper bound
is retained. Preserve /var/db/sdwdate across reboots. Corrupt checkpoints
fail closed; do not casually delete them to suppress validation errors.

Native test checklist
=====================
Check rcctl, ps aux, fstat -p <pid>, ls -ln of the runtime directory/cookie,
and syslog. Use netstat -an -f unix and fstat; sockstat is not OpenBSD base.
Verify no root Python process, no public privileged socket, no inherited
broker descriptor in HTTP children, and no setuid bits.

Test stop/restart/SIGTERM, Tor unavailable and bootstrapping, control timeout,
bad SAFECOOKIE permissions, reconnect, invalid and duplicate Date headers,
partial pool failure, empty pools, and all-source failure. No failure may
produce a successful clock status. Test simultaneous starts and ntpd conflict.

Only in a disposable OpenBSD VM, with ntpd stopped, test positive/negative
initial corrections, +/-gradual corrections, --slew-only rejection, excessive
offset rejection, helper failure, parent SIGKILL, child failure, reboot,
checkpoint recovery and hardware-clock persistence. Confirm no orphaned clock
writer, no abrupt step in gradual mode, and correct normalized nanoseconds.
Do not perform these clock-changing tests on the Fedora analysis host.
